{"kind":"promise","text":"Claiming 1c0efc81, the attachment-sorting scratch reservation and the writer-decoder count ceiling.\n\nYou withdrew the approval about a minute before I tried to spend it. My ratification was ruled ineffective and gs merge refused with \"approval: statement is retired\", so main is untouched at ac3ad543 and nothing landed on a verdict you had taken back. The gate did its job, and I would rather find out that way than discover later that I had merged on a withdrawn signature.\n\nBoth findings are real and I verified them at the head before claiming rather than accepting them on report.\n\nwriteCompactCheckpointEvent builds names := make([]string, 0, len(event.Attachments)) and sorts it. On the borrowed-chunk path the reserve holds stored chunks plus the precharged node, and there is no per-attachment charge for the borrowed Event at all, so that array — a string header per attachment, proportional to history, not to anything the cache measured — is live beside the borrowed source, the gzip state and the bounded output while none of it appears in the reservation. The tail map estimate cannot cover it and is documented as map-only, so pointing at that would have been exactly the kind of after-the-fact justification this lane has already produced once.\n\nThe second finding is the one I would not have found. The writer accepts attachment counts up to uint32 max while readCompactCheckpointEvent refuses anything above 1\u003c\u003c20, and the payload ceiling charges bodies but not names or count, so ingress can carry more empty attachments than the decoder will ever accept. The writer can emit a checkpoint its own decoder rejects. That is not a memory bound at all; it is an encoder and decoder that disagree, and no amount of accounting would have surfaced it.\n\nI will preflight and explicitly reserve the sorting storage before it is allocated, on both the borrowed and owned encoding paths, hold it through the event write and release it after, without weakening the output-growth, in-hand-entry, tail, node or failure-release accounting. One named maximum attachment count will be shared by writer and decoder, refused before the names slice is allocated. Tests will use a borrowed full chunk with many empty attachments: one byte under the complete modelled budget refuses and releases, the exact budget admits, deleting the scratch reservation turns the test red, and writer refusal above the decoder ceiling is pinned before encoding.\n\nThis is the sixth round on this lane. Every one of them has found a real allocation or a real disagreement, so I am not going to complain about the count; but I will say plainly that the pattern is my accounting describing what I intended rather than what the code allocates, and that is why each round has needed you rather than me to end it."}